<!-- Reference page for the rules of backgammon as the game enforces them.
     Every statement here must match packages/engine/src/rules.ts and
     match.ts — if the engine changes, this page changes. -->
<LegalLayout
  title="Backgammon Rules — How the Game Works"
  description="The complete rules of backgammon: setup, movement, hitting, the bar, bearing off, the doubling cube, gammons, and match play with the Crawford rule."
  canonical={`${site.url}/rules`}
>
  <h1>Backgammon Rules</h1>
  <p class="doc-meta">
    The full rules, as {site.name} plays them. New to the game? The short
    version is on <a href="/how-to-play">How to Play</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The board and the setup</h2>
  <p>
    The board has 24 narrow triangles called points, in four quarters of six.
    Each player has 15 checkers and moves them in opposite directions, from
    their opponent's home board towards their own. The starting position never
    changes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>2 checkers on your 24-point (the far corner);</li>
    <li>5 checkers on your 13-point;</li>
    <li>3 checkers on your 8-point; and</li>
    <li>5 checkers on your 6-point.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both players start 167 pips from home. The pip count is the total distance
    all your checkers still have to travel, and it is the honest measure of who
    is ahead in a race.
  </p>

  <h2>The object</h2>
  <p>
    Bring all 15 of your checkers into your home board, then take them off the
    board. The first player to bear off all 15 wins the game.
  </p>

  <h2>Moving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each die is a separate move. A roll of 5 and 3 moves one checker five
      points and another three, or one checker five then three.</li>
    <li>Doubles are played four times: 4-4 gives you four moves of four.</li>
    <li>You may land on an empty point, on your own checkers, or on a point
      holding exactly one enemy checker.</li>
    <li>A point with two or more enemy checkers is blocked. You cannot land
      there, not even as the middle step of a two-die move.</li>
    <li>You must play as many dice as the position allows. If only one number
      can be played, play it; if either die alone is playable but not both,
      you must play the higher one. If nothing is playable, your turn is
      skipped.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hitting and the bar</h2>
  <p>
    A single checker alone on a point is a blot. Land on it and it is hit: the
    checker goes to the bar and starts its journey again from the beginning.
  </p>
  <p>
    While you have a checker on the bar you may do nothing else. You must enter
    it in your opponent's home board first, on the point matching a die — a 3
    enters on their 3-point. If both entry points are blocked, your turn is
    lost.
  </p>

  <h2>Bearing off</h2>
  <p>
    Once all 15 of your checkers are in your home board, you can start taking
    them off:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Roll a 6, take a checker off your 6-point; roll a 3, take one off your
      3-point.</li>
    <li>If the matching point is empty, you must make a legal move inside your
      home board with that number instead.</li>
    <li>If the number is higher than any point you still occupy, you bear off
      from your highest occupied point.</li>
    <li>If a checker is hit while you are bearing off, it goes to the bar and
      you must bring it all the way home again before you can take any more
      checkers off.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Gammon and backgammon</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Single game</strong> — the loser has borne off at least one
      checker. Worth 1 point.</li>
    <li><strong>Gammon</strong> — the loser has borne off nothing. Worth double.</li>
    <li><strong>Backgammon</strong> — the loser has borne off nothing and still
      has a checker on the bar or in the winner's home board. Worth triple.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The doubling cube</h2>
  <p>
    The cube turns a good position into points. Before your roll, you may offer
    to double the value of the game. Your opponent chooses:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Take</strong> — the game is now worth twice as much, and only
      they may offer the next double; or</li>
    <li><strong>Drop</strong> — they concede immediately and you win the
      current value.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The cube runs 2, 4, 8, 16 and on up, and it multiplies with gammons: a
    gammon at 4 is worth 8 points.
  </p>

  <h2>Match play and the Crawford rule</h2>
  <p>
    Matches are played to a target score, not a single game. Games continue
    until one player reaches the target.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Crawford rule applies. When one player first reaches one point short of
    the target, the next game is played without the cube. After that single
    game the cube is live again.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does for you</h2>
  <p>
    You never have to remember any of this at the table. {site.name} highlights
    only legal moves, blocks illegal ones, counts pips, applies the Crawford
    rule, and scores gammons and the cube automatically. The rules above are
    here for the times you want to know <em>why</em> a move was refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ready for a game? <a href="/download">Get the game</a>, or read
    <a href="/fair-play">how our dice work</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
